  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 845 PM MST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nCool temperatures will persist through the night as a surface high\nsettles over the Texas Panhandle, keeping prevailing winds blowing from\nthe east. Heading into Friday, the GFS and ECMWF continue to \ntrend towards the upper-level trough moving through the Rockies \ntransitioning into a closed low over Arizona, allowing more \nmoisture to be pulled up from northern Mexico. PWAT levels are \nexpected to be around 0.6\" for Friday, which is not sufficient for\nsoaking rains given limited instability, though total rainfall \namounts are expected to be light to moderate; areas east of the \nRio Grande favored to see rainfall totals near half an inch into \nSaturday, especially Hudspeth County.\n\nThe progression of rain showers will start from southwestern New \nMexico Friday afternoon before moving east along the US-Mexico \nborder Friday evening. Rain will linger over east of El Paso into \nSaturday (about a 70-90% chance). Widespread cloud cover and a \nweak backdoor cold front sliding in from the east Friday evening \nwill keep lowland high temperatures stuck in the upper 50s for \nSaturday (or about 10-15 degrees below average). Portions of the \nSacramento Mountains could see a wintry mix or snow on Saturday as\ntemperatures hang around the low to mid 30s, though accumulation \nis unlikely.\n\nRain chances dwindle on Sunday as the low moves east, though\nHudspeth County will see some lingering showers. Temperatures\nbegin their gradual warmup on Sunday as drier air moves in.\nTemperatures will return to normal on Wednesday. Looking ahead,\nboth the GFS and ECMWF are in agreement that conditions will be\ndry through midweek, with both runs showing a pronounced upper- \nlevel ridge over our area on Thursday, though we will continue to \nmonitor for any changes.",
